.TH LIGHTTABLE 1
.SH NAME
lighttable \- a two-dimensional optics bench
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/lighttable
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I LightTable
is a port of the TempleOS
.B Demo/Graphics/LightTable.HC
demonstration.
It runs under
.IR wm (1).
.PP
Draw mirrors and lenses, then fire photons at them and watch them
reflect and refract.
.PP
Two ideas hold it up.  The optics live in a bitmap rather than in a list
of shapes, so a spline, a freehand line and a flood-filled blob are all
the same thing to a photon.  And the surface normal is recovered from
that bitmap by geometry alone \- back the photon up a step and walk it
forward at the fine bitmap's resolution to find where it touched, then
sweep two arcs out from the backward direction, coarse to fine, for the
two points where the surface crosses a small circle, and take the
perpendicular of the chord \- with no stored gradients and no analytic
curve, which is why anything you can draw refracts correctly.  Between
them the arcs cover the whole circle, which matters: on a grazing hit the
crossings lie either side of the way the photon is going, and a search
that misses them sends it back the way it came.
.PP
The fine bitmap is at a larger scale than the display so that search has
sub-pixel detail to work with, which is what the original's opening
comment is about.  It also holds outlines only, where the coarse map is
what gets flood-filled, so that the interior of a lens is not itself a
surface.  Sixteen megabytes of it, as the original defaults to, is about
fifteen fine cells to the display pixel at 640 by 480.
.PP
Reflection is one line and refraction is Snell's law with an index of 1.5
inside a lens.  A photon that has just bent is stopped from bending again
within two pixels of where it did, which is what keeps it from rattling
along a surface it has only just met.  Photons bounce off the border and
are never destroyed.
.PP
Measured against the exact laws: reflection off a straight mirror is
within 0.9 degrees at every angle of incidence from 7 to 79 degrees, and
refraction into a flood-filled lens within 0.15 degrees from 5 to 69.
.PP
The controls are:
.TF "any key"
.TP
.B 0-5
Drawing mode: mirror line, mirror spline, lens line, lens spline, lens
fill, test ray
.TP
.B LMB
Draw, or fire a burst in test-ray mode
.TP
.B .
End a spline
.TP
.B r
Two hundred and fifty-six photons at random
.TP
.B n
Show the normals
.TP
.B Enter
Clear
.TP
.B q
Exit
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/temple/lighttable.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR wm (1)
